WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.171 --> 00:00:01.004
(crowd applause)
- All right, everybody.

00:00:01.004 --> 00:00:02.939
We're back with Randy
Jackson and Kate Walsh.

00:00:02.939 --> 00:00:03.863
Give it up.

00:00:03.863 --> 00:00:06.751
(crowd applause)

00:00:06.751 --> 00:00:08.543
Well, since he first entered the pros,

00:00:08.543 --> 00:00:10.373
our next guest has been considered one

00:00:10.373 --> 00:00:13.530
of the greatest lacrosse
players on the planet.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:15.031
Bur bur bur bur,
(audience laughs)

00:00:15.031 --> 00:00:17.751
He's also known as a huge
rebel within the sport,

00:00:17.751 --> 00:00:19.111
which is super fun.

00:00:19.111 --> 00:00:21.613
After 10 years in Major League Lacrosse,

00:00:21.613 --> 00:00:24.413
he was appalled with the way
players were being treated.

00:00:24.413 --> 00:00:28.213
So in 2018, he and his brother
took on the establishment.

00:00:28.213 --> 00:00:31.154
They created a new league
that promised better pay,

00:00:31.154 --> 00:00:33.733
better conditions, even health benefits.

00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:36.773
His ambitious, often emotional
journey was documented

00:00:36.773 --> 00:00:39.463
in the new film co-produced
by LeBron James.

00:00:39.463 --> 00:00:41.314
Never heard of him.
(audience laughter)

00:00:41.314 --> 00:00:42.389
Fate of a Sport.

00:00:42.389 --> 00:00:45.056
(intense music)

00:00:46.575 --> 00:00:49.845
- Starting a professional
sports league is impossible.

00:00:49.845 --> 00:00:50.678
- On the field-

00:00:50.678 --> 00:00:52.277
You shouldn't be standing
for this (bleep).

00:00:52.277 --> 00:00:54.007
- I don't see them doing a deal with us.

00:00:54.007 --> 00:00:56.257
- It's hard for me to
imagine what it would be

00:00:56.257 --> 00:01:01.247
like to both be a commissioner
and play in the NBA.

00:01:01.247 --> 00:01:02.080
- I know he's got a lot on his
plate, you know what I mean?

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:03.575
(players chanting)

00:01:03.575 --> 00:01:05.508
You're running a league,

00:01:05.508 --> 00:01:08.920
you're trying to win a championship,

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:11.111
and you're trying to
perform as an individual

00:01:11.111 --> 00:01:14.340
- How (bleep) hard is that?

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:15.173
(swoosh)

00:01:15.173 --> 00:01:16.006
(audience applause)

00:01:16.006 --> 00:01:18.251
- That's co-founder of the
Premier Lacrosse League,

00:01:18.251 --> 00:01:21.020
a National and International
Lacrosse Champion,

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:21.903
Paul Rabil.

00:01:21.903 --> 00:01:22.736
Give it up.

00:01:22.736 --> 00:01:24.930
(audience applause)

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:25.763
- Thank you.

00:01:25.763 --> 00:01:27.004
- Well, first of all, meet Randy.

00:01:27.004 --> 00:01:27.972
Meet Kate.

00:01:27.972 --> 00:01:28.805
- Hey, guys.

00:01:28.805 --> 00:01:29.763
- Hello. (laughs)

00:01:29.763 --> 00:01:30.596
- Such a pleasure.

00:01:30.596 --> 00:01:31.429
- Dude, nice to meet you.

00:01:31.429 --> 00:01:32.262
Great sport, Lacrosse.

00:01:32.262 --> 00:01:33.675
- Thank you. Thank you. Appreciate that.

00:01:33.675 --> 00:01:34.803
- Tough.

00:01:34.803 --> 00:01:35.636
- It is a tough sport.

00:01:35.636 --> 00:01:37.031
So tell us, tell us more about

00:01:37.031 --> 00:01:39.631
why you wanted to launch
a new lacrosse league.

00:01:39.631 --> 00:01:41.001
- Well, actually tough,

00:01:41.001 --> 00:01:43.651
In the late 1960s, Sports
Illustrated had lacrosse

00:01:43.651 --> 00:01:45.961
on the cover, and they
called it the tough game.

00:01:45.961 --> 00:01:48.852
But, well, well before that,
it was a Native American game.

00:01:48.852 --> 00:01:51.652
It's actually the first sport
in North American history.

00:01:51.652 --> 00:01:53.025
It was created by the
Haudenosaunee people.

00:01:53.025 --> 00:01:53.858
It was called-

00:01:53.858 --> 00:01:54.691
- What?

00:01:54.691 --> 00:01:56.993
- Deyhontsigwa'ehs, which
means "they bump hips,"

00:01:56.993 --> 00:02:01.294
And it was created to celebrate
and honor the creator.

00:02:01.294 --> 00:02:04.825
The version that we see
today is the secular version

00:02:04.825 --> 00:02:07.764
and it's the game that
was once in the Olympics.

00:02:07.764 --> 00:02:10.375
Fast forward to me growing up in the 90's

00:02:10.375 --> 00:02:12.047
and playing in 2000's, it just no longer

00:02:12.047 --> 00:02:15.735
had those roots or that
purpose or that soul.

00:02:15.735 --> 00:02:17.575
And as I played for 12 years in the pros

00:02:17.575 --> 00:02:21.535
formerly with Major League
Lacrosse, my wage was $8,000.

00:02:21.535 --> 00:02:23.695
We had no marketing, we
had no network partner,

00:02:23.695 --> 00:02:28.695
and I was just feeling like the
sport shouldn't be this way.

00:02:28.768 --> 00:02:31.168
And you saw MLS, UFC, F1.

00:02:31.168 --> 00:02:32.958
Now, we all watched them at the time,

00:02:32.958 --> 00:02:35.299
but they were on the same journey.

00:02:35.299 --> 00:02:38.699
So if you take on the internet,
modern media and stuff,

00:02:38.699 --> 00:02:41.179
those new leagues have since emerged.

00:02:41.179 --> 00:02:42.978
And I felt like lacrosse could do that.

00:02:42.978 --> 00:02:44.550
So I partnered with my brother.

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:46.468
We took on the establishment.

00:02:46.468 --> 00:02:49.468
It was a hell of a ride, and
it brought me here today.

00:02:49.468 --> 00:02:51.237
- Wow.
(audience applause)

00:02:51.237 --> 00:02:52.070
- That is incredible.

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:54.638
I did not know the, like,
origin story of lacrosse.

00:02:54.638 --> 00:02:55.471
It's really cool.

00:02:55.471 --> 00:02:56.304
I did not know that.

00:02:56.304 --> 00:02:57.689
And I will be googling that afterwards.

00:02:57.689 --> 00:02:58.522
- So cool.

00:02:58.522 --> 00:02:59.355
- Cause I'm-

00:02:59.355 --> 00:03:00.188
- I did my job here then.

00:03:00.188 --> 00:03:01.021
- Yeah

00:03:01.021 --> 00:03:01.854
(laughs)

00:03:01.854 --> 00:03:03.067
- We are gonna go away and study.

00:03:03.067 --> 00:03:05.248
Well, starting a new
professional sports league

00:03:05.248 --> 00:03:08.013
that can't be like a small
task, that's daunting.

00:03:08.013 --> 00:03:09.253
- Yeah, there were two things really.

00:03:09.253 --> 00:03:12.972
So my brother and I, who
is my co-founder of the PLL

00:03:12.972 --> 00:03:15.412
we have major imposter syndrome.

00:03:15.412 --> 00:03:16.702
I think, you know, a lot

00:03:16.702 --> 00:03:18.112
of people may be able to relate to that.

00:03:18.112 --> 00:03:19.413
You have that as an athlete.

00:03:19.413 --> 00:03:20.246
- Yeah.

00:03:20.246 --> 00:03:21.310
- And then build that over the top of

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:22.901
there was already a league that we were

00:03:22.901 --> 00:03:24.681
ultimately fighting against.

00:03:24.681 --> 00:03:25.514
- Yeah.

00:03:25.514 --> 00:03:28.441
- So there were lawsuits
over top of even, you know

00:03:28.441 --> 00:03:32.740
signing players, building
teams, getting venues done,

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:35.863
legal, marketing, promotion,
all this stuff that

00:03:35.863 --> 00:03:38.223
neither my brother and I
had a background in sports.

00:03:38.223 --> 00:03:42.460
So we were taken on for the
first time and then we launched

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:45.610
and then Major League Lacrosse
came back to us and said,

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:46.930
"All right, you've done that.

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:47.998
Now we're ready to buy you."

00:03:47.998 --> 00:03:51.288
And they offered us $35
million for the league

00:03:51.288 --> 00:03:53.968
which Mike and I had never
seen anything like that.

00:03:53.968 --> 00:03:55.409
- Well, that's a lot of money.

00:03:55.409 --> 00:03:56.488
(laughs)

00:03:56.488 --> 00:03:57.321
Yeah.

00:03:57.321 --> 00:03:58.154
- [Kate] It's a lot of money.

00:03:58.154 --> 00:03:59.468
- That's hard to, to pass on.

00:03:59.468 --> 00:04:00.301
It's a lot of shoes.

00:04:00.301 --> 00:04:01.219
- Yeah. (laughs)

00:04:01.219 --> 00:04:04.156
- I wish I had the gumption to sort of get

00:04:04.156 --> 00:04:06.228
on the phone and say, "It's
gonna be a no for me, dawg."

00:04:06.228 --> 00:04:07.061
- Yeah. (laughs)

00:04:07.061 --> 00:04:07.894
- Oh, look at him.

00:04:07.894 --> 00:04:08.727
Look at him, Kel, Look
at him, look at him.

00:04:08.727 --> 00:04:09.560
(laughing)

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:10.946
Oh my God, I use that GIF all the time!

00:04:10.946 --> 00:04:11.779
- That was less, I was less smooth.

00:04:11.779 --> 00:04:13.304
I was less smooth than that.

00:04:13.304 --> 00:04:14.586
I was less smooth.

00:04:14.586 --> 00:04:17.775
But, we knew, we knew
that it wasn't, you know

00:04:17.775 --> 00:04:20.553
while there would've
been a short-term payoff,

00:04:20.553 --> 00:04:23.386
our mission was to not
just build a better future

00:04:23.386 --> 00:04:27.434
for lacrosse players, but
realign the game with its roots.

00:04:27.434 --> 00:04:28.267
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:28.267 --> 00:04:29.100
- [Randy] Love that.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:30.563
- But you just started a program

00:04:30.563 --> 00:04:31.955
called Goals for Greatness.

00:04:31.955 --> 00:04:33.101
So what is this about?

00:04:33.101 --> 00:04:36.471
- Yeah, so this is sort
of outside of the PLL.

00:04:36.471 --> 00:04:39.282
I just announced my retirement
a couple of years ago.

00:04:39.282 --> 00:04:41.782
Trying to play and build
the league was just too much

00:04:41.782 --> 00:04:44.331
of a conflict for me personally.

00:04:44.331 --> 00:04:47.009
And I thought about ways that lacrosse

00:04:47.009 --> 00:04:49.050
could potentially reach new people

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:51.021
and we can visually see it.

00:04:51.021 --> 00:04:52.258
So what I committed to do in

00:04:52.258 --> 00:04:54.357
Goals for Greatness is to put goals

00:04:54.357 --> 00:04:56.677
and fields in all 50 states every year.

00:04:56.677 --> 00:04:57.826
I started it last year.

00:04:57.826 --> 00:05:00.738
So we contributed goals to all 50 states.

00:05:00.738 --> 00:05:03.446
We host free clinics
for our program partners

00:05:03.446 --> 00:05:05.017
that we work with as you can see here.

00:05:05.017 --> 00:05:07.406
Our first one was with Compton
Lacrosse here in California.

00:05:07.406 --> 00:05:08.886
That was last March.

00:05:08.886 --> 00:05:10.636
And as we start 2023,

00:05:11.547 --> 00:05:13.835
we just notified Oakland
Lacrosse earlier today

00:05:13.835 --> 00:05:15.545
in the Bay Area that we're
gonna contribute 10 goals

00:05:15.545 --> 00:05:16.606
to their program.

00:05:16.606 --> 00:05:17.883
So we're gonna start in California

00:05:17.883 --> 00:05:18.716
and then we're gonna go hopefully

00:05:18.716 --> 00:05:20.669
in future years internationally.

00:05:20.669 --> 00:05:21.886
(audience applause)
- Great.

00:05:21.886 --> 00:05:23.161
- [Kelly] That's so cool. Opportunity-

00:05:23.161 --> 00:05:24.058
- Nice.

00:05:24.058 --> 00:05:25.359
- It's such an important thing.

00:05:25.359 --> 00:05:26.690
I literally, I was involved in the arts

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:29.189
and the athletic programs
and it's so important

00:05:29.189 --> 00:05:32.149
even to get the endorphins,
you know, everything going.

00:05:32.149 --> 00:05:33.170
It's so beneficial.

00:05:33.170 --> 00:05:35.085
- Yeah. I think the values that we get

00:05:35.085 --> 00:05:37.114
from sports or the arts,

00:05:37.114 --> 00:05:40.843
and I've had my own journey
with my competitive nature,

00:05:40.843 --> 00:05:43.994
I would venture to say that
none of you three would be here

00:05:43.994 --> 00:05:45.854
if you weren't supreme competitors.

00:05:45.854 --> 00:05:46.687
- I'm competitive.

00:05:46.687 --> 00:05:47.520
(laughs)

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:48.952
- I would bet.
(laughs)

00:05:48.952 --> 00:05:50.805
- In a healthy way.
- In a way, I'm, I am.

00:05:50.805 --> 00:05:53.024
I'm not about, but when it comes
to, remember, when it comes

00:05:53.024 --> 00:05:54.534
to work, I'd be like, why are
all these other people here

00:05:54.534 --> 00:05:55.556
for my role?

00:05:55.556 --> 00:05:56.389
- Right, exactly.
- You know what I mean?

00:05:56.389 --> 00:05:57.222
(laughs)
(claps)

00:05:57.222 --> 00:05:58.055
(audience laughs)

00:05:58.055 --> 00:05:58.888
- It was was like almost sociopathic,

00:05:58.888 --> 00:06:00.403
but I literally I was like,

00:06:00.403 --> 00:06:01.444
- (laughs) Sounds like you had
a healthy relationship, yeah.

00:06:01.444 --> 00:06:02.385
"Why are they signed in on the,

00:06:02.385 --> 00:06:04.054
you know we used to audition."
- How dare you.

00:06:04.054 --> 00:06:04.905
- [Kate] Yeah, yeah.

00:06:04.905 --> 00:06:06.274
Why are you here?
Sorry, guys.

00:06:06.274 --> 00:06:07.107
Go home.
I got this.

00:06:07.107 --> 00:06:07.940
(audience laughs)

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:10.590
- But I think when you
start out, to me, Louisiana

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:11.948
you want to be the best.

00:06:11.948 --> 00:06:13.148
- [Paul] Yeah.

00:06:13.148 --> 00:06:14.960
- You want to shoot for the top, top, top.

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:15.902
All of you.

00:06:15.902 --> 00:06:16.735
All of us.

00:06:16.735 --> 00:06:17.568
- Yeah.

00:06:17.568 --> 00:06:18.401
- That's what we did.

00:06:18.401 --> 00:06:19.234
- Yeah, we're very lucky.

00:06:19.234 --> 00:06:20.067
- Thank you so much, Paul.

00:06:20.067 --> 00:06:20.900
- My pleasure.

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:21.733
I loved all the information.

00:06:21.733 --> 00:06:22.600
It's powerful.
(audience applause)

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:23.433
- Thank you.

00:06:23.433 --> 00:06:26.393
- Fate of a Sport is streaming
now on Hulu and ESPN.

